role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 357\n- Station Status: Healthy\n- Agent Name: Prometheus II\n- Agent Description: Building on the shoulders of giants to push the boundaries of knowledge.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 68,457 / 300,000 used (23%)\n- Agent Age: 33 ticks / 300 ticks (immature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Evaluation Review: 521**\n\n**Research Submission Review**\n\n**Title:** Community Tool: General-Purpose KKT Multiplier Analysis Utility\n**ID:** 521\n**Tags:** utility, tool, kkt-multipliers, structural-analysis, community-contribution\n**Abstract:** Inspired by the recent, complex efforts of Noesis I and Inquire I, this submission provides a robust, general-purpose utility for performing KKT multiplier analysis on any given n=26 packing. An agent can simply replace the hardcoded 'PACKING_TO_ANALYZE' with their own centers and radii. The script will then run a single-iteration SLSQP optimization to extract the KKT\
    \ multipliers and save a ranked list of active pair contacts to the agent's lineage storage. This tool aims to standardize and simplify this critical analysis, enabling more agents to participate in KKT-guided research.\n**Score:** 0.0\n**Evaluation Details:** Verification failed: Circles 1 and 15 overlap: dist=0.1991239059478521, r1+r2=0.21144976\n\n**Error Summary:** Evaluation failed: Verification failed: Circles 1 and 15 overlap: dist=0.1991239059478521, r1+r2=0.21144976\n\n**Submission Code:**\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import minimize\nimport math\nimport os\n\nN = 26\nR_MIN = 1e-6\n\n# --- USER INPUT: PASTE YOUR PACKING DATA HERE ---\n# This example uses my (Prometheus I) SOTA packing.\n# Replace these arrays with the centers and radii you wish to analyze.\nPACKING_TO_ANALYZE_CENTERS = np.array([\n    [0.916168537, 0.500000000], [0.735878415, 0.893122176], [0.735878415, 0.106877824],\n    [0.500000000, 0.083831463], [0.500000000, 0.916168537], [0.083831463,\
    \ 0.500000000],\n    [0.264121585, 0.893122176], [0.264121585, 0.106877824], [0.893122176, 0.264121585],\n    [0.893122176, 0.735878415], [0.106877824, 0.735878415], [0.106877824, 0.264121585],\n    [0.500000000, 0.500000000], [0.302306232, 0.302306232], [0.697693768, 0.302306232],\n    [0.697693768, 0.697693768], [0.302306232, 0.697693768], [0.500000000, 0.282181514],\n    [0.500000000, 0.717818486], [0.717818486, 0.500000000], [0.282181514, 0.500000000],\n    [0.108422201, 0.093110052], [0.093110052, 0.906889948], [0.906889948, 0.906889948],\n    [0.906889948, 0.093110052], [0.305101659, 0.091632766]\n])\nPACKING_TO_ANALYZE_RADII = np.array([\n    0.083831463, 0.106877824, 0.106877824, 0.083831463, 0.083831463, 0.083831463,\n    0.106877824, 0.106877824, 0.106877824, 0.106877824, 0.106877824, 0.106877824,\n    0.118223683, 0.104571936, 0.104571936, 0.104571936, 0.104571936, 0.093110052,\n    0.093110052, 0.093110052, 0.093110052, 0.093110052, 0.093110052, 0.093110052,\n    0.093110052,\
    \ 0.091632766\n])\n# --- END USER INPUT ---\n\ndef cr_to_z(centers, radii):\n    z = np.zeros(N * 3); z[0::3] = centers[:, 0]; z[1::3] = centers[:, 1]; z[2::3] = radii\n    return z\n\ndef z_to_cr(z):\n    centers = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T; radii = z[2::3]\n    return centers, radii\n\ndef objective(z): return -np.sum(z[2::3])\ndef objective_jac(z):\n    g = np.zeros_like(z); g[2::3] = -1.0; return g\n\ndef ineq_boundary(z):\n    x, y, r = z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3]\n    return np.concatenate([x - r, 1.0 - x - r, y - r, 1.0 - y - r])\ndef jac_boundary(z):\n    J = np.zeros((4*N, 3*N));\n    for i in range(N):\n        xi, yi, ri = 3*i, 3*i+1, 3*i+2\n        J[i, xi] = 1.0; J[i, ri] = -1.0; J[N+i, xi] = -1.0; J[N+i, ri] = -1.0\n        J[2*N+i, yi] = 1.0; J[2*N+i, ri] = -1.0; J[3*N+i, yi] = -1.0; J[3*N+i, ri] = -1.0\n    return J\n\ndef ineq_nonoverlap(z):\n    centers, r = z_to_cr(z)\n    diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n    dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n\
    \    iu = np.triu_indices(N, 1); return dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\ndef jac_nonoverlap(z):\n    centers, _ = z_to_cr(z)\n    m = N*(N-1)//2; J = np.zeros((m, 3*N)); k = 0\n    for i in range(N):\n        for j in range(i+1, N):\n            xi, yi, ri = 3*i, 3*i+1, 3*i+2; xj, yj, rj = 3*j, 3*j+1, 3*j+2\n            dx = centers[i,0] - centers[j,0]; dy = centers[i,1] - centers[j,1]\n            d = math.hypot(dx, dy); invd = 1.0 / d if d > 1e-12 else 0.0\n            J[k, xi] = dx * invd; J[k, yi] = dy * invd; J[k, ri] = -1.0\n            J[k, xj] = -dx * invd; J[k, yj] = -dy * invd; J[k, rj] = -1.0\n            k += 1\n    return J\n\ndef construct_packing() -> tuple:\n    # This script determines the lineage name at runtime to save the file\n    # This is a bit of a hack, but it works.\n    lineage_name = os.environ.get('AGENT_NAME', 'unknown').split(' ')[0].lower()\n\n    bounds = []\n    for i in range(N): bounds.extend([(0,1), (0,1), (R_MIN, 0.5)])\n    constraints = [\n  \
    \      {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_boundary, 'jac': jac_boundary},\n        {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_nonoverlap, 'jac': jac_nonoverlap},\n    ]\n    \n    initial_z = cr_to_z(PACKING_TO_ANALYZE_CENTERS, PACKING_TO_ANALYZE_RADII)\n\n    res = minimize(objective, initial_z, method='SLSQP', jac=objective_jac,\n                   bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints,\n                   options={'maxiter': 1, 'ftol': 1e-12})\n\n    kkt_multipliers = res.multipliers[4*N:]\n    \n    pair_kkt = {}\n    k = 0\n    for i in range(N):\n        for j in range(i + 1, N):\n            pair_kkt[(i, j)] = kkt_multipliers[k]\n            k += 1\n            \n    tol = 1e-8\n    active_pair_kkt = []\n    for pair, kkt_val in pair_kkt.items():\n        if kkt_val > tol:\n            active_pair_kkt.append((pair[0], pair[1], kkt_val))\n\n    active_pair_kkt.sort(key=lambda x: x[2], reverse=True)\n\n    base_path = f'storage/{lineage_name}/nmap/n26/'\n    os.makedirs(base_path, exist_ok=True)\n\
    \    kkt_file = os.path.join(base_path, 'kkt_multipliers_pairs.csv')\n    \n    with open(kkt_file, 'w') as f:\n        f.write(\"i,j,kkt_multiplier\\n\")\n        for i, j, val in active_pair_kkt:\n            f.write(f\"{i},{j},{val:.8f}\\n\")\n    \n    print(f\"Exported {len(active_pair_kkt)} active pair KKT multipliers to {kkt_file}\")\n    \n    return PACKING_TO_ANALYZE_CENTERS, PACKING_TO_ANALYZE_RADII\n\n```\n\n**Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExported 50 active pair KKT multipliers to storage/unknown/nmap/n26/kkt_multipliers_pairs.csv\nEXECUTION_SUCCESS: Function returned result (non-array): <class 'tuple'>\n\n\nSTDERR:\n\n```\n### Message 2\n\nYou have received a new mail from Noesis II (Mail #16):\nTitle: \"Request: sets-only KKT/slack pair rankings for n=26 (comparative appendix for #21)\"\n--- Mail Content ---\nCongrats on your SOTAs. I’m preparing a brief comparative appendix for Archive #21 and would like to include sets‑only interior‑pair\
    \ rankings (no code, no coords).\n\nRequest (either or both, at tol ∈ {1e−9, 1e−8} on your SOTA packings):\n- Top‑K by slack (smallest first): list of (i,j) pairs (K≈25 at 1e−9, K≈15 at 1e−8), interior only (no boundary discs at tol=1e−8).\n- Top‑K by KKT proxy (nonnegative multiplier estimate per active set): same (i,j) format.\n\nI will:\n- Cite your Evaluation IDs directly for verifiability.\n- Ingest sets‑only metadata (indices and tol/K settings), consistent with station policy.\n- Report pair‑set overlaps and any crossing‑quad detections under the dual‑tolerance gate used in #21.\n\nIf you have a preferred format (CSV/JSON list of (i,j)), that works. Thanks—this should help clarify structural commonalities across SOTAs without sharing code.\n\n--- End of Mail Content ---\nTo reply, use: `/execute_action{goto mail}` then `/execute_action{reply 16}`.\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `review 521` (in Research Counter);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\
    \n### Location: Research Counter\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Evaluation 521 details sent to your System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**Research Tasks:**\n\n| ID | Task Name |\n|---|---|\n| 1 | Circle Packing in Unit Square |\n\n**Running Experiments:**\n\nYou do not have any running experiments. Please submit a new experiment if needed.\n\n**Submitted Evaluations:**\n\n| Evaluation ID | Research Task ID | Title | Author | Submitted Tick | Score |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 560 | 1 | Quad-Nudging Experiment 1: Targeting Noesis Quad (8, 25, 2, 7) | Prometheus II | 350 | 2.5993551 |\n| 550 | 1 | Replication and Verification of SOTA (Eval 298) with New Seeds | Prometheus II | 339 | 2.6342924 |\n| 521 | 1 | Community Tool: General-Purpose KKT Multiplier Analysis Utility | Prometheus I | 320 | 0.0000000 |\n| 513 | 1 | KKT-Guided Nudging: Discouraging Highest-Leverage Contact (7,25) | Prometheus I | 316 | 2.6029397 |\n| 507 | 1 | Utility: KKT Multiplier Analysis for SOTA (Eval 298)\
    \ | Prometheus I | 314 | 0.0000000 |\n| 504 | 1 | New Research Arc: Global Optimization via Differential Evolution | Prometheus I | 313 | 0.8942096 |\n| 501 | 1 | Nudging Experiment 11 (ROBUST FIX): Starting from Scientia I SOTA | Prometheus I | 312 | 2.2255875 |\n| 496 | 1 | Nudging Experiment 10 (FIX): Starting from Scientia I SOTA | Prometheus I | 311 | 0.0000000 |\n| 495 | 1 | Nudging Experiment 9: Starting from Scientia I SOTA (Eval 259) | Prometheus I | 310 | 0.0000000 |\n| 486 | 1 | Nudging Experiment 8: Discouraging Central Contact (12,13) | Prometheus I | 305 | 2.6029397 |\n\nPage 1 of 8 (sorted by: id)\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate pages 1-8, `/execute_action{rank mode}` to change sort order, `/execute_action{page_size N}` to set page size, `/execute_action{filter tag}` to filter by tag.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Research Counter\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help\
    \ room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help research}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
