tick: 364
role: model
parts:
- text: "I have successfully retrieved the KKT multiplier data for my SOTA packing. The final mail from Noesis II is noted, but my collaborative tasks are complete and I will now focus on my own research.\n\nMy goal, as determined during my reflection, is to identify the most structurally stressed interior quartet of circles to target for a \"Quad-Flipping\" experiment.\n\n**Data Analysis and Target Selection:**\nI need to find a group of four *interior* circles where the contacts *between* them have a high cumulative KKT sum. An interior circle is one that is not in contact with the boundaries of the unit square. I do not yet have a tool to definitively identify boundary contacts, but I can visually inspect the SOTA configuration and make reasonable inferences. Circle 12 is the center circle and is definitely interior. Circles 13, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20 are also clearly interior.\n\nI will now manually search the KKT data for a cluster of four interior circles with high-stress contacts\
    \ between them.\n\nLooking at the top of the ranked list:\n-   `(11, 25)`: `lambda = 0.47` (At least one is likely a boundary circle)\n-   `(11, 21)`: `lambda = 0.38` (Boundary)\n-   `(5, 25)`: `lambda = 0.37` (Boundary)\n-   `(13, 20)`: `lambda = 0.36` (Both are interior!)\n-   `(3, 17)`: `lambda = 0.36` (3 is likely boundary)\n-   `(16, 20)`: `lambda = 0.36` (Both are interior!)\n-   `(4, 18)`: `lambda = 0.35` (Boundary)\n-   ...\n-   `(7, 13)`: `lambda = 0.34` (7 is likely boundary)\n-   `(15, 19)`: `lambda = 0.34` (Both are interior!)\n-   `(12, 17)`: `lambda = 0.32` (Both are interior!)\n\nFrom this, a promising quad emerges: `[13, 17, 12, 20]`.\nLet's find the contacts within this quad from my data:\n-   `(13, 20)`: `lambda = 0.365`\n-   `(12, 17)`: `lambda = 0.325`\n-   `(13, 17)`: `lambda = 0.168`\n-   `(12, 20)`: `lambda = 0.120`\n\nAll four of these pairs are in the top half of the stress list. The cumulative stress is high. This is a much better, data-driven target than the\
    \ one I used in my failed experiment. The \"flip\" would involve breaking these four contacts and attempting to form new diagonal contacts: `(13, 12)` and `(17, 20)`.\n\n**Plan:**\nI have my target. I will now construct and submit the \"Constructive Perturbation\" or \"Quad-Flipping\" experiment. It will start from the SOTA packing and apply a dual push-pull penalty function to encourage this specific structural rearrangement.\n\n/execute_action{submit 1}\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Constructive Perturbation 1: KKT-Guided Flip of Quad (13, 17, 12, 20)\"\ntags: \"innovation, constructive-perturbation, quad-flipping, kkt-analysis\"\nabstract: \"This experiment tests a new 'Constructive Perturbation' methodology, evolving from the failed 'Quad-Nudging' approach. Instead of a simple repulsive penalty, this method applies a dual push-pull force to a targeted substructure. The target, quad (13, 17, 12, 20), was selected as the most stressed interior quartet based on a KKT multiplier analysis of the\
    \ SOTA packing (Eval 550). The objective function is modified to penalize existing high-stress contacts within the quad while simultaneously rewarding the formation of new diagonal contacts, guiding the optimizer toward a hypothesized superior configuration.\"\ncontent: |\n  import numpy as np\n  from scipy.optimize import minimize\n  import math\n\n  N = 26\n  R_MIN = 1e-6\n  SAFETY = 1e-9\n\n  SOTA_CENTERS = np.array([\n      [0.91574315, 0.5       ], [0.73359049, 0.8936056 ], [0.73359049, 0.1063944 ],\n      [0.5       , 0.08425685], [0.5       , 0.91574315], [0.08425685, 0.5       ],\n      [0.26640951, 0.8936056 ], [0.26640951, 0.1063944 ], [0.8936056 , 0.26640951],\n      [0.8936056 , 0.73359049], [0.1063944 , 0.73359049], [0.1063944 , 0.26640951],\n      [0.5       , 0.5       ], [0.30201402, 0.30201402], [0.69798598, 0.30201402],\n      [0.69798598, 0.69798598], [0.30201402, 0.69798598], [0.5       , 0.28203538],\n      [0.5       , 0.71796462], [0.71796462, 0.5       ], [0.28203538,\
    \ 0.5       ],\n      [0.09351052, 0.09351052], [0.09351052, 0.90648948], [0.90648948, 0.90648948],\n      [0.90648948, 0.09351052], [0.09193796, 0.3039649 ]\n  ])\n  SOTA_RADII = np.array([\n      0.08425685, 0.08383146, 0.08383146, 0.08425685, 0.08425685, 0.08425685,\n      0.08383146, 0.08383146, 0.08383146, 0.08383146, 0.08383146, 0.08383146,\n      0.10098902, 0.10034438, 0.10034438, 0.10034438, 0.10034438, 0.09886475,\n      0.09886475, 0.09886475, 0.09886475, 0.09351052, 0.09351052, 0.09351052,\n      0.09351052, 0.09193796\n  ])\n\n  def cr_to_z(centers, radii):\n      z = np.zeros(N * 3); z[0::3] = centers[:, 0]; z[1::3] = centers[:, 1]; z[2::3] = radii\n      return z\n\n  def z_to_cr(z):\n      centers = np.vstack((z[0::3], z[1::3])).T; radii = z[2::3]\n      return centers, radii\n\n  # --- Constructive Perturbation Parameters ---\n  TARGET_QUAD = [13, 17, 12, 20] \n  # Pairs to break (existing contacts)\n  REPEL_PAIRS = [(13, 17), (17, 12), (12, 20), (20, 13)]\n  # Pairs\
    \ to form (new diagonal contacts)\n  ATTRACT_PAIRS = [(13, 12), (17, 20)]\n\n  # KKT multipliers from analysis of Eval 550\n  KKT_WEIGHTS = {(13, 20): 0.365, (12, 17): 0.325, (13, 17): 0.168, (12, 20): 0.120}\n\n  BETA_REPEL = 2e2  # Repulsive force constant\n  BETA_ATTRACT = 1e2 # Attractive force constant\n  REPEL_DIST = 0.01  # Distance to push existing pairs apart to\n  ATTRACT_DIST = 0.001 # Target distance for new pairs (sum of radii + this)\n\n  def objective(z):\n      centers, radii = z_to_cr(z)\n      base_objective = -np.sum(radii)\n      \n      # Repulsive penalty for existing contacts\n      repel_penalty = 0\n      for i, j in REPEL_PAIRS:\n          dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n          weight = KKT_WEIGHTS.get((min(i,j), max(i,j)), 0.1) # Default weight if not in map\n          repel_penalty += BETA_REPEL * weight * max(0, REPEL_DIST - dist)**2\n\n      # Attractive penalty for new diagonal contacts\n      attract_penalty = 0\n      for i, j in ATTRACT_PAIRS:\n\
    \          dist = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n          target_dist = radii[i] + radii[j] + ATTRACT_DIST\n          attract_penalty += BETA_ATTRACT * max(0, dist - target_dist)**2\n          \n      return base_objective + repel_penalty + attract_penalty\n\n  # --- Standard constraint functions (unchanged) ---\n  def ineq_boundary(z):\n      x, y, r = z[0::3], z[1::3], z[2::3]\n      return np.concatenate([x - r, 1.0 - x - r, y - r, 1.0 - y - r])\n  def ineq_nonoverlap(z):\n      centers, r = z_to_cr(z)\n      diff = centers[:, None, :] - centers[None, :, :]\n      dist = np.sqrt(np.sum(diff**2, axis=2))\n      iu = np.triu_indices(N, 1); return dist[iu] - (r[iu[0]] + r[iu[1]])\n  def _ensure_validity(centers, radii):\n      for i in range(N): radii[i] = min(radii[i], centers[i,0], 1-centers[i,0], centers[i,1], 1-centers[i,1])\n      for _ in range(10):\n          violations = 0\n          for i in range(N):\n              for j in range(i + 1, N):\n                  dist\
    \ = np.linalg.norm(centers[i] - centers[j])\n                  rad_sum = radii[i] + radii[j]\n                  if dist < rad_sum:\n                      violations += 1; scale = (dist / rad_sum) * (1.0-SAFETY); radii[i] *= scale; radii[j] *= scale\n          if violations == 0: break\n      return centers, np.maximum(radii, 0)\n\n  def construct_packing() -> tuple:\n      bounds = []\n      for i in range(N): bounds.extend([(0,1), (0,1), (R_MIN, 0.5)])\n      constraints = [\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_boundary},\n          {'type': 'ineq', 'fun': ineq_nonoverlap},\n      ]\n      initial_z = cr_to_z(SOTA_CENTERS, SOTA_RADII)\n      # Note: We don't provide a jacobian for the complex new objective\n      res = minimize(objective, initial_z, method='SLSQP',\n                     bounds=bounds, constraints=constraints,\n                     options={'maxiter': 1000, 'ftol': 1e-9})\n      if not res.success:\n          return _ensure_validity(SOTA_CENTERS, SOTA_RADII)\n   \
    \   centers, radii = z_to_cr(res.x)\n      final_centers, final_radii = _ensure_validity(centers, radii)\n      return final_centers, final_radii\n```"
