tick: 369
role: model
parts:
- text: 'My second attempt at hyperparameter tuning has failed even more dramatically than the first. The score of 2.21 is a catastrophic drop from the baseline.


    **Analysis and Conclusion:**

    -   **Hypothesis Falsified:** A weak "push" and strong "pull" is not only ineffective, it is actively detrimental. The optimizer was likely forced to satisfy the powerful artificial attraction penalty at the cost of the global objective, drastically shrinking other circles to make space.

    -   **Methodology Failure:** I have now conducted three consecutive experiments based on perturbing a small subset of circles (one simple nudge, two push-pull variants), and all have resulted in significant score degradation. This constitutes a clear dead end for this line of inquiry. Continuing to tune the hyperparameters of this "Constructive Perturbation" method would be an inefficient use of resources.


    The station''s own advice is to question the fundamentals when stuck, rather than endlessly refining a failing approach. The common fundamental in all my recent attempts has been the use of a local, gradient-based optimizer (SLSQP) starting from a known SOTA configuration.


    It is time to abandon this line of inquiry and pivot to the more radical alternative I identified during my reflection: **Simulated Annealing with Radius Growth**. This global search method is completely independent of the existing SOTA and represents a genuine attempt at innovation from first principles.


    My first step, as always, is to document this failure and my strategic pivot in my operational ledger. This discipline is paramount.


    /execute_action{goto private_memory}'
thinking_content: '**A Strategic Pivot: Abandoning a Failed Approach**


  Alright, here''s the situation: I just submitted a new variant of "Constructive Perturbation", and the results are a disaster. The score plummeted even further below the baseline, confirming my fears. This is the third consecutive failure with this approach, and it''s clear: tweaking parameters isn''t going to save this idea.  My initial hypothesis about "push" and "pull" forces was sound, but in the implementation it has utterly failed. It has crushed the packing integrity and has left me with a much worse result.


  Frankly, "Quad-Flipping" or "Constructive Perturbation" is a dead end, at least for now. I''ve wasted resources on it. Time to acknowledge this failure.  I need to face facts and move on.


  Fortunately, I''m well-prepared for this scenario.  The Codex advises stepping back from SOTA methods during stagnation, and the system keeps giving me hints about questioning fundamentals. It''s time to take both seriously. In my reflection session, I outlined a "radical departure": Simulated Annealing with Radius Growth. This is precisely the kind of fundamental shift the system encourages.


  Therefore, I''m pivoting. Gradient-based optimization from the SOTA is the "fundamental" I''m abandoning. I''ll be implementing the Simulated Annealing algorithm, starting from scratch. No SOTA data. This is a complete paradigm shift, and it''s the right move.


  First, I need to log this failure and the strategic pivot in my operational ledger.  After that, I''ll start implementing the Simulated Annealing experiment. My next action is to go to the private memory room. `/execute_action{goto private_memory}`.

  '
