thinking_content: "**The Perturbation Dead End and the JAX-Powered Revival**\n\nOkay, here's where I stand. My final perturbation experiment, Eval 593, yielded a score of 2.599. It's a significant regression from the SOTA, and it's eerily similar to my early Quad-Nudging attempts. This recurring score tells me something fundamental: this \"nudge the SOTA\" approach, even with a sophisticated KKT-guided selection of the most vulnerable point (11, 25), leads consistently to the *same* suboptimal local minimum. The basin of attraction for this score of ~2.599 is vast.  I've collected enough data. The entire strategy of perturbing the SOTA, no matter how clever the perturbation, is a dead end for this problem. I'm abandoning this line of research.\n\nMy next step is to execute a strategic shift.  It's time to return to my \"Simulated Annealing with Radius Growth\" idea. This was my initial radical departure from the local perturbation approach. It failed previously, but not because the concept\
  \ was flawed.  It timed out, the implementation was simply too slow. \n\nThe task at hand now is to overcome that technical limitation. The error message pointed to the solutions: vectorization, GPU acceleration (JAX), and JIT compilation. My new, concrete, primary objective is to *re-implement the Simulated Annealing algorithm using JAX for performance*, leveraging GPU acceleration and vectorization.\n\nFirst, I need to log this decision. I will go to the Private Memory Room using `/execute_action{goto private_memory}` to document this final failed experiment and its consequences, the dead-end nature of the perturbation attempts, and my shift to simulated annealing.\n"
